<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk" ToolsVersion="15.0" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AssemblyName>Microsoft.TestPlatform.CoreUtilities</AssemblyName>
    <TargetFrameworks>net7.0;net6.0;netstandard2.0;$(NetFrameworkMinimum);$(NetCoreAppMinimum)</TargetFrameworks>
    <IsTestProject>false</IsTestProject>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition=" '$(TargetFramework)' == '$(NetFrameworkMinimum)' ">
    <Reference Include="System.Configuration" />
    <Reference Include="System" />
    <Reference Include="System.IO" />
    <Reference Include="System.Runtime" />
    <Reference Include="System.Reflection" />
    <Reference Include="Microsoft.CSharp" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition=" '$(TargetFramework)' != '$(NetFrameworkMinimum)' ">
    <PackageReference Include="System.Diagnostics.FileVersionInfo" Version="4.3.0" />
    <!-- netcoreapp3.1's Microsoft.NETCore.App declares Microsoft.Win32.Registry as a framework-provided
         package, so a 4.0.0 reference is suppressed and nothing reaches the compile line; the ref pack's
         type forwards then resolve to nothing and every Registry type fails with CS1069. 5.0.0 is above
         that override, so the compile asset survives. Only this TFM is raised - the others build today
         and their package content must stay equivalent to the shipped 17.12 artifacts.
         COPIED FROM 17.8 AND 17.11, WHERE IT IS PROVEN: 17.8's build 3067220 took `Build` from "fails at
         the C# compiler" to `succeeded` with exactly this change, and 17.11's 3067302 measured CS1069
         18 -> 0. 17.12 carries the IDENTICAL 4.0.0 pin and the IDENTICAL TargetFrameworks list, measured
         on this branch, so it hits the same defect. Deliberately NOT 17.14's mechanism, which conditions
         the reference to netstandard2.0 only and would leave netcoreapp3.1 with nothing. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Win32.Registry" Version="4.0.0" Condition=" '$(TargetFramework)' != '$(NetCoreAppMinimum)' " />
    <!-- ExcludeAssets="runtime": the 5.0.0 COMPILE asset is what fixes CS1069, but its RUNTIME asset
         must not reach vstest.console.deps.json. Without this, deps.json demands
         runtimes/win/lib/netstandard2.0/Microsoft.Win32.Registry.dll, Microsoft.TestPlatform.CLI does
         not contain it, and vstest.console fails to START on netcoreapp3.1. MEASURED ON THIS LINE, not
         inferred from 17.11: build 3067419's acceptance run stopped at 672/1105 and the TRX names
         "package: 'Microsoft.Win32.Registry', version: '5.0.0' path:
         'runtimes/win/lib/netstandard2.0/Microsoft.Win32.Registry.dll'" with Runner = netcoreapp3.1,
         against Microsoft.TestPlatform.CLI.17.12.0-release-26455-13.nupkg. Shipped 17.11.1's CLI was
         measured to carry ZERO runtimes/ entries and to list Registry with no runtime section at all,
         so excluding the runtime asset RESTORES the shipped shape rather than changing it.
         netcoreapp3.1's shared framework provides the assembly at runtime. Port of 17.11's 5e2ea42ac.

         PrivateAssets MUST be "all". A narrower list is MEASURED insufficient across FOUR builds
         spanning both lines, all byte-for-byte identical to their own control on the acceptance
         assembly: 17.11 3067428 (control, no attribute), 3067436 (ExcludeAssets="runtime"),
         3067475 (plus PrivateAssets="runtime;contentfiles;analyzers;build") all gave
         690 Passed / 325 Failed / 90 NotExecuted out of 1105; 17.12 3067429 (control) and
         3067480 (this file's previous revision) both gave 672 / 343 / 90. Identical numbers across
         four different asset configurations mean none of them changed the artifact.

         What the produced artifact actually says, read out of the nupkg build 3067475 emitted:
         contentFiles/any/netcoreapp3.1/vstest.console.deps.json lists Microsoft.Win32.Registry/5.0.0
         with BOTH a runtime section (lib/netstandard2.0) AND a runtimeTargets section
         (runtimes/win/lib/netstandard2.0), while the package ships NEITHER file - a recursive scan
         of the extracted CLI nupkg finds zero files matching Win32.Registry. Shipped 17.11.1's CLI
         lists Microsoft.Win32.Registry/4.0.0 with no runtime and no runtimeTargets section at all,
         which is why it starts. The defect is therefore the 4.0.0 -> 5.0.0 bump making the SDK stop
         treating the package as framework-provided, not an asset attribute on the reference.

         CORRECTION, MEASURED ON BUILD 3067504 - THE VERSION MATTERS AND 5.0.0 WAS THE WRONG ONE.
         PrivateAssets="all" DID work: the deps.json entry is gone and the old "assembly specified in
         the application dependencies manifest was not found" error is gone with it. It UNMASKED the
         real one. Acceptance moved 672/343/90 -> 680 Passed / 335 Failed / 90 NotExecuted - a move of
         exactly 8, the same move 17.11 made on 3067499 - and 159 of the 335 surviving failures name:
           System.IO.FileNotFoundException: Could not load file or assembly
           'Microsoft.Win32.Registry, Version=5.0.0.0, Culture=neutral, PublicKeyToken=b03f5f7f11d50a3a'.
         So the assembly is now resolved from the shared framework, as intended - but the COMPILE-TIME
         IDENTITY baked in by package 5.0.0 is 5.0.0.0, and .NET rolls assembly versions UP only, never
         DOWN. A netcoreapp3.1 shared framework carries 4.1.3.0, so 5.0.0.0 can never be satisfied.
         MEASURED package -> assembly version: 4.0.0 -> 4.0.0.0, 4.4.0 -> 4.1.0.0, 4.7.0 -> 4.1.3.0,
         5.0.0 -> 5.0.0.0. And the framework PackageOverrides entry is Microsoft.Win32.Registry|4.4.0
         (read out of Microsoft.NETCore.App.Ref 6.0.36 and 8.0.27 on the dev box), which is why 4.0.0
         is suppressed and gives CS1069.
         4.7.0 is the ONE version that satisfies BOTH constraints: it is ABOVE the 4.4.0 override so the
         compile asset survives and CS1069 stays fixed, and its identity 4.1.3.0 is exactly what a 3.1
         framework provides and is BELOW every modern one (6.0.0.0 / 8.0.0.0 / 9.0.0.0 / 10.0.0.0), so
         it rolls up everywhere. Shipped 17.12's CLI compiled against 4.0.0 -> 4.0.0.0 for the same
         reason. Port of 17.11's 95b5d90d6, whose own build was queued as 3067529.

         The narrower list failed because it omits "compile". The compile asset still flowed across
         the ProjectReference, which put the library into vstest.console's graph, and the deps.json
         emitter then wrote the library's FULL asset set, runtimeTargets included. Suppressing
         "runtime" alone cannot help once the library is in the graph. "all" keeps the package
         entirely private to this project, so the compile asset still fixes CS1069 here and nothing
         reaches vstest.console. Verified safe: no source file outside this project references
         Microsoft.Win32, so no consumer needs the compile asset. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Win32.Registry" Version="4.7.0" ExcludeAssets="runtime" PrivateAssets="all" Condition=" '$(TargetFramework)' == '$(NetCoreAppMinimum)' " />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="System.Reflection.Metadata" Version="1.6.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Update="NullableHelpers.cs">
      <DesignTime>True</DesignTime>
      <AutoGen>True</AutoGen>
      <DependentUpon>NullableHelpers.tt</DependentUpon>
    </Compile>
    <Compile Update="Resources\Resources.Designer.cs">
      <DesignTime>True</DesignTime>
      <AutoGen>True</AutoGen>
      <DependentUpon>Resources.resx</DependentUpon>
    </Compile>
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Update="Resources\Resources.resx">
      <Generator>ResXFileCodeGenerator</Generator>
      <LastGenOutput>Resources.Designer.cs</LastGenOutput>
    </EmbeddedResource>
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Microsoft.TestPlatform.PlatformAbstractions\Microsoft.TestPlatform.PlatformAbstractions.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- API that is common to all frameworks that we build for. -->
    <AdditionalFiles Include="PublicAPI/PublicAPI.Shipped.txt" />
    <AdditionalFiles Include="PublicAPI/PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt" />
    <AdditionalFiles Include="PublicAPI\net\PublicAPI.Shipped.txt" />
    <AdditionalFiles Include="PublicAPI\net\PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt" />
    <!-- Framework specific API -->
    <AdditionalFiles Condition=" '$(TargetFramework)' != 'net6.0' and '$(TargetFramework)' != 'net7.0' " Include="PublicAPI/$(TargetFramework)/PublicAPI.Shipped.txt" />
    <AdditionalFiles Condition=" '$(TargetFramework)' != 'net6.0' and '$(TargetFramework)' != 'net7.0' " Include="PublicAPI/$(TargetFramework)/PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt" />
    <!-- Framework specific API for Net6.0 and Net7.0 -->
    <AdditionalFiles Condition=" '$(TargetFramework)' == 'net6.0' or '$(TargetFramework)' == 'net7.0' " Include="PublicAPI/net6.0_and_net7.0/PublicAPI.Shipped.txt" />
    <AdditionalFiles Condition=" '$(TargetFramework)' == 'net6.0' or '$(TargetFramework)' == 'net7.0' " Include="PublicAPI/net6.0_and_net7.0/PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\..\shared\NullableAttributes.cs" Link="NullableAttributes.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Update="NullableHelpers.tt">
      <LastGenOutput>NullableHelpers.cs</LastGenOutput>
      <Generator>TextTemplatingFileGenerator</Generator>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Service Include="{508349b6-6b84-4df5-91f0-309beebad82d}" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <PropertyGroup Label="Configuration">
    <RootNamespace>Microsoft.VisualStudio.TestPlatform.CoreUtilities</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
